WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.220
Once upon a time, it was rare to see a character
straight-up return from the dead.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:08.980
Existing in narrative limbo under a shroud
of post-story ambiguity, like what happened

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:11.580
to Breaking Bad's Walter White or Tony Soprano
in The Sopranos?

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:12.720
Sure.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:14.600
But to come entirely back?

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.960
That was highly unlikely.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:20.460
It was even less likely in action films like
the Fast and the Furious franchise, which

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:25.009
don't necessarily lend themselves to the same
high-concept, rule-bending universe-building

00:00:25.009 --> 00:00:28.090
of science-fiction, fantasy, and superhero
genres.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:33.240
And yet, the first trailer for the ninth Fast
and Furious movie, F9, teased just that.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:38.309
The final few seconds of the trailer effectively
rose fan-favorite character Han from the grave.

00:00:38.309 --> 00:00:42.609
Han made his first appearance in the Fast
franchise in 2006's The Fast and the Furious:

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:46.930
Tokyo Drift, the same film in which he was
believed to have died in a fiery car crash.

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:52.260
In the F9 trailer, he's seen walking through
a corridor, snack in hand as always, and then

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.260
there's this:

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:54.650
"Nice clubhouse."

00:00:58.660 --> 00:00:59.200
Whoa!

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:00.760
That's unexpected.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:05.110
The big reveal has many fans excited — and
it should, because Han's return to the Fast

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:07.980
and Furious film series is due in large part
to them.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:13.150
In a recent interview with Entertainment Weekly,
F9 director Justin Lin explained that after

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:18.570
he departed from the Fast franchise back in
2013, a new story idea coupled with comments

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:23.470
from hardcore fans inspired him to come back,
bringing his long-time collaborator and Han

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:25.330
actor Sung Kang with him.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:26.320
He told EW,

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:30.260
"I would travel and just meet people and fans
of the franchise, and they would tell me why

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:32.980
they love the franchise so much and just share
their stories.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:37.240
And along the way, I hadn't seen the other
two movies, and I was at a Q&amp;A for Better

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.720
Luck Tomorrow and someone brought up 'justice
for Han,' and so all these things were kind

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:42.560
of working together."

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.500
The #JusticeforHan hashtag was started by
fans angry over the franchise's decision to

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:52.490
turn former Fast and Furious villain Deckard
Shaw into an ally of Dom Torretto's crew before

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:55.440
then giving him and Lucas Hobbs a 2019 spin-off.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.360
In the sixth Fast installment, Shaw was revealed
to be behind Han's presumed death in Tokyo

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:00.900
Drift.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.080
Oddly enough, Han's F9 appearance will basically
serve as his second resurrection.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:10.320
In a 2016 interview with EW celebrating the
10th anniversary of Tokyo Drift, both Lin

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.880
and actor Sung Kang said the story of the
nomadic, silent observer with deep pockets

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.140
actually goes all the way back to the director's
first feature film.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.760
In Fast Five, it's established that the name
"Han Seoul-Oh", with "Seoul" spelled like

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:27.020
the capital of South Korea, is an alias — and
an obvious reference to Han Solo from Star

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:27.540
Wars.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:32.500
Lin subsequently confirmed in various interviews
that Han is actually a continuation of Han

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:37.000
Lue, the chain-smoking partier with a moral
compass from his 2002 independent crime drama

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.500
Better Luck Tomorrow.

00:02:38.829 --> 00:02:43.780
Based on the real-life 1992 "honor roll slaying,"
the film follows a group of high-achieving

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:47.800
Asian American high school students who swap
suburban boredom for a school-wide cheating

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.880
operation that takes a dark turn.

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:54.060
Lin and Kang have grown alongside this character
— which is why when the director departed

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.080
from the franchise, it felt right to finally
let the character go.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:01.659
But now Lin is saying that both he and Han
have unfinished business in the flashy Fast

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:02.670
and Furious universe.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:04.920
He told EW in January 2020,

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:08.860
"When I left, I felt it was appropriate, and
I felt like we were putting the character

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:12.100
to bed, but it's because of some of the things
that happened that didn't quite make sense

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:16.340
to me, and so I felt like if I was going to
come back, I really wanted to explore why."

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:21.660
It's still unclear how, exactly, Han will
make his return, but we'll know soon enough.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.980
F9 races into theaters on May 22.

